Here’s how to get a Google Photos API key / access setup (important: Google Photos mainly uses OAuth, not just a simple API key).

⸻

📸 Step 1: Go to Google Cloud Console

Open: Google Cloud Console
https://console.cloud.google.com/

⸻

🧱 Step 2: Create a Project
	•	Click Select Project → New Project
	•	Name it (e.g., Photos App)
	•	Click Create

⸻

🔌 Step 3: Enable Google Photos API
	•	Go to APIs & Services → Library
	•	Search: Google Photos Library API
	•	Click → Enable

👉 Entity: Google Photos Library API

⸻

🔐 Step 4: Choose the Right Credential Type

⚠️ This is where many people get confused:

❌ API Key (NOT sufficient for Photos)
	•	Google Photos does NOT work properly with just an API key
	•	You cannot access user photos with only an API key

⸻

✅ Use OAuth 2.0 (Required)

Steps:
	1.	Go to APIs & Services → Credentials
	2.	Click Create Credentials → OAuth client ID
	3.	Configure consent screen:
	•	App name
	•	User support email
	4.	Choose application type:
	•	Web app / Desktop app
	5.	Create → Copy:
	•	Client ID
	•	Client Secret

👉 Entity: OAuth 2.0

⸻

🔑 Step 5: Scopes (Important)

When requesting access, use scopes like:

https://www.googleapis.com/auth/photoslibrary.readonly

or

https://www.googleapis.com/auth/photoslibrary


⸻

💻 Example Flow (Simple Concept)
	1.	User logs in with Google
	2.	User grants permission to Photos
	3.	You get access token
	4.	Use token to call API

⸻

🧪 Example API Call

curl https://photoslibrary.googleapis.com/v1/albums \
  -H "Authorization: Bearer ACCESS_TOKEN"


⸻

🍏 Optional: Store credentials as env variables (macOS)

export GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID="your_client_id"
export GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET="your_client_secret"


⸻

🧠 Summary
	•	Google Photos API ❌ does NOT use simple API keys
	•	You MUST use:
	•	OAuth 2.0
	•	Access tokens
	•	API key alone = only for limited public data (not user photos)

